<section class="wrap page-head">
  <p class="page-head__eyebrow">About</p>
  <h1>About Carney Journal</h1>
</section>

<section class="wrap prose">
  <p>
    Carney Journal is my personal publication for original writing, statistical analysis,
    and side projects. I'm a first-year college student, and I started this site to have
    one place to publish work as I make it, rather than letting it sit unfinished on my
    laptop.
  </p>

  <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
  <p>
    The first project is a rating system that scores college football players on a 1–10
    scale using a statistical distribution I built myself. Over time I expect to write
    about other things I'm interested in — architecture, coding, statistics, and whatever
    else pulls my attention.
  </p>

  <h2>How I label work</h2>
  <p>
    This is not peer-reviewed academic research, and I don't want it to look like it is.
    Every article carries one of three labels so you know how finished it is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Working Paper</strong> — an early idea, still being tested. Methods and conclusions may change.</li>
    <li><strong>Draft</strong> — mostly formed, but not fully checked or polished yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Finished</strong> — reviewed and settled, though I may still revise it later if I learn something new.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you spot an error or a better way to do something, I'd genuinely like to hear about
    it — see the <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it's free to read</h2>
  <p>
    Everything on this site is free. If you enjoy the work and want to support it, there's
    a <a href="/donate/">Donate page</a> with a way to chip in — entirely optional.
  </p>
</section>
